### Description

Sometimes environments need to communicate between each other: For example a server-rendered app might want to tell the browser to reload a certain page when the code that renders that page changes. Other use cases include frameworks telling browser code to reload some data when the server code providing that data changes.

### Suggested solution

Implement an `import.meta.hot.sendTo(environmentName, eventName, data)` method to send messages between arbitrary environments. For example, the data refetching use case could do something like:

```ts
if (import.meta.hot) {
	import.meta.hot.accept(() => {
		import.meta.hot!.sendTo("client", "awesome-framework:reload-data", { path: "/foo" });
	});
}
```

with a corresponding listener on the client to handle refetching the data.

### Alternative

Implementing similar functionality in a plugin is not hard:

```ts
{
	name: "cross-env-send",
	configureServer(server) {
		server.environments[sourceEnv].hot.on("cross-env-send", (payload) => {
			server.environments[payload.env].hot.send(
				payload.event,
				payload.data,
			);
		});
	},
}
```

but to me it seems like a fundamental feature that should live in the core so that the whole framework ecosystem can benefit from a standardized way of doing it.

